= The Fourth Congress

The Fourth Congress of the Communist International closes in Moscow on 5 December, after a month of speeches. Lenin, grey and partly paralysed since his first stroke, spoke for an hour on 13 November, and said the International had passed a resolution too Russian for foreigners to understand. Few of the delegates expect to hear him again.

The great question for the German delegation was the **workers' government**. Radek and Ernst Meyer argued that a government of the workers' parties, even one formed in a parliament, could be a step towards power: if the SPD would break with the bourgeoisie, communists could sit beside its ministers. Ruth Fischer answered that a workers' government could only be another name for the dictatorship of the proletariat, and that anything else was coalition politics in a red shirt. Zinoviev's resolution allowed for both, and five kinds of workers' government in between.

[? if e22_fc_directive = 1 : Radek drafted the instruction to the German section himself, and it leaves the Zentrale four months to show results: the united front in the factories, and a workers' government where one can be had. ?] With Lenin ill, Zinoviev chairs, Radek writes, Trotsky lectures, and a quiet general secretary called Stalin answers the letters.

[? if e22_workers_gov = 2 : Our own ministers in Berlin make the debate less theoretical than the delegates would like. ?][? if saxony_government = 1 : Our ministers in Dresden make the debate less theoretical than the delegates would like. ?]

The delegation must say what the German party will take home.

- @broad: Take home Radek's formula: a workers' government can begin in parliament.
- @narrow: Take home the Left's formula: no coalition, only the dictatorship of the proletariat.
- @own: Declare that the German party will apply it to German conditions.
- @zinoviev: Accept the resolution as written, every word of it.

@broad
subtitle: The Right's line. The road to Saxony and to coalition offers is open.
on-arrival: {!
Q.e22_fourth_congress = 1;
RG.comply(35);
RG.ecci('radek', 8);
RG.ecci('lenin', 4);
RG.ecci('zinoviev', -2);
Q.unionist_satisfaction += 8;
Q.offensive_satisfaction -= 8;
Q.lean_unionist += 3;
Q.spd_relation += 3;
!}

The Zentrale's report to the Central Committee puts it plainly: the party will offer the SPD a workers' government wherever the two parties have a majority, and fight for it where they do not.

@narrow
subtitle: The Left's line. The radicals are encouraged; the SPD shrugs.
view-if: offensive_gone = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.e22_fourth_congress = 2;
RG.comply(10);
RG.ecci('zinoviev', 6);
RG.ecci('bukharin', 6);
RG.ecci('radek', -8);
Q.offensive_satisfaction += 8;
Q.unionist_satisfaction -= 8;
Q.lean_offensive += 3;
Q.spd_relation -= 3;
Q.militancy += 3;
!}

Fischer returns to Berlin in triumph. The workers' government, she tells the district, is a slogan for the street, not for the Landtag.

@own
subtitle: The Spartacist answer. More independence; Moscow will want to know what it means.
on-arrival: {!
Q.e22_fourth_congress = 3;
Q.independence += 8;
Q.luxemburgist_satisfaction += 7;
Q.lean_luxemburgist += 2;
RG.stall(2);
RG.ecci('zinoviev', -8);
RG.ecci('radek', -3);
!}

[? if luxemburg_alive = 1 : Rosa Luxemburg, who did not go to Moscow, writes the German resolution herself. ?][? if luxemburg_alive = 0 : Clara Zetkin's report insists that the German party will choose its own allies. ?] Zinoviev calls it a lapse into federalism, and lets it pass.

@zinoviev
subtitle: Obeys Moscow word for word. Unity rises; independence falls.
on-arrival: {!
Q.e22_fourth_congress = 4;
RG.comply(40);
RG.ecci('zinoviev', 10);
Q.independence -= 7;
Q.unity += 4;
Q.moscow_subsidy += 1;
!}

The delegation votes for the resolution in full. At home every current reads in it exactly what it hoped to find.
